Finally, one can make an extension beyond leading twist not by including transverse motion, but adding a gluon to the quark correlation matrix. This case is known as the twist-3 case. This can be investigated in [21, 22, 23, 24, 25]. In principle, the twist-3 cross section is rewritten with the inclusion of the gluon term and a twist-3 fragmentation function is defined. There are three important terms in this new expression, the initial state twist-3 effect, the transversity dependent term and the final state twist-3 effect. It turns out that the transversity dependent term is negligible [25] and that the first and the third term can give rise to the asymmetries. In fact, it is proven that moments of transverse momentum dependent distribution functions (specifically the Sivers function) are interrelated with the matrix elements of the quark-gluon correlators [29]. Also, there are other possibilities for structure functions and fragmentation functions that can give rise to the asymmetries. The field is large, and a nice experimental and theoretical overview can be found in [30].

