are produced with an energy spectrum \(N_{\pi^{0}}(E_{\pi^{0}},\theta_{\pi^{0}})\) then the energy spectrum of the decay photons in the detector centered on \(\theta_{\pi^{0}}\) is

\[N_{\gamma}(E_{\gamma},\theta_{\gamma}=\theta_{\pi^{0}})=\int\limits_{E_{\gamma} }^{\infty}\frac{2}{E_{\pi^{0}}}N_{\pi^{0}}(E_{\pi^{0}},\theta_{\pi^{0}}){\rm d }E_{\pi^{0}}\,. \tag{7.21}\]

Taking the derivative, one finds

\[N_{\pi}(E_{\pi},\theta_{\pi})=-\frac{E_{\pi}}{2}\frac{{\rm d}N_{\gamma}(E_{ \gamma}=E_{\pi},\theta_{\gamma}=\theta_{\pi})}{{\rm d}E_{\gamma}}\,. \tag{7.22}\]

So, if one knows the distribution of energies of the photons, it can be directly connected to the distribution of the pions.

